Element Use for <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> Main page heading <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> Major section <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code> Subsection <code>&lt;h4&gt;</code> Smaller subsection <code>&lt;h5&gt;</code> Deeper subsection <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code> Lowest level <p>The takeaway:</p> <p>Heading levels describe what your content is, not how big it should look.</p> <p>A tidy page often looks like:</p> <pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;Page Topic&lt;/h1&gt;
    │
    ├── &lt;h2&gt;Section&lt;/h2&gt;
    │       ├── &lt;h3&gt;Subsection&lt;/h3&gt;
    │       └── &lt;h3&gt;Subsection&lt;/h3&gt;
    │
    └── &lt;h2&gt;Section&lt;/h2&gt;
            └── &lt;h3&gt;Subsection&lt;/h3&gt;
</code></pre> <p>Use them to make your content easier to understand, navigate and maintain, for you, for search engines, and for the next person who reads your code.</p>

Element / Attribute Purpose <code>&lt;q&gt;</code> Short, inline quotation <code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code> Longer, standalone quotation <code>&lt;cite&gt;</code> Title or source of a work <code>cite</code> attribute URL of the source <p>Example with both:</p> <pre><code>&lt;p&gt;
    She said, &lt;q&gt;Learning never stops.&lt;/q&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
    Education is the most powerful weapon which you can use
    to change the world.
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
</code></pre>
